// test-execution
// test-no-warnings
// Issue #1450: `this.<member>` string assignment resolves by its QUALIFIED
// name, and two scopes may declare the same member name at different widths.
//
// WHAT THIS PINS, AND WHY THE SNAPSHOT IS THE ASSERTION. `string-assign-
// scope-this` already reaches this handler -- it is the only fixture that
// does -- but it declares ONE scope, so the qualified key `Logger__message`
// and the bare key `message` cannot disagree. They disagree here.
//
// The type registry is keyed on the qualified name and holds `message` for
// neither scope bare; the bare name still RESOLVES, through the symbol-table
// fallback `getVariableTypeInfo` falls back to, and that fallback answers with
// whichever `message` it finds. Replacing the qualified key with the bare one
// therefore does not fail -- it silently sizes `Narrow`'s copy from `Wide`'s
// capacity:
//
//     (void) strncpy(Narrow__message, "narrow", 64); Narrow__message[64] = '\0';
//
// `Narrow__message` is `char[9]`. That is a 55-byte out-of-bounds write in the
// generated C, emitted with no diagnostic and exit 0. The `.expected.c` beside
// this file carries the bound 8, which is what fails when the qualification is
// dropped; `char_count` alone would NOT catch it, since the copied text is
// short either way and the smash lands in adjacent storage.
//
// `Unique.banner` is the negative control: a scope member whose name no other
// scope shares. A change that stopped qualifying, or one that over-qualified
// into a key nothing holds, has to keep this one working too.
scope Wide {
    public string<64> message <- "";
    public void set() {
        this.message <- "wide";
    }
}

scope Narrow {
    public string<8> message <- "";
    public void set() {
        this.message <- "narrow";
    }
}

scope Unique {
    public string<16> banner <- "";
    public void set() {
        this.banner <- "unique";
    }
}

u32 main() {
    Wide.set();
    if (Wide.message.char_count != 4) return 1;

    Narrow.set();
    if (Narrow.message.char_count != 6) return 2;
    if (Wide.message.char_count != 4) return 3;

    Unique.set();
    if (Unique.banner.char_count != 6) return 4;

    return 0;
}
